{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1462 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3e35ea3507128915279dda078a811e27c98ac61dc1b7b42bed57c684da1e51f2" :edb/db-before-t 1461 :edb/db-after-t 1462 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534774 50 #inst "2026-10-12T12:02:56.204Z" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186228922 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 false] [17592186228922 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186229191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435667 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186229191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435668 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186229252 1050 1140.0 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186229680 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 false] [17592186229680 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186230184 1064 "Consider the integral\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the integration variable and $t$ is a parameter. How do we compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} I}{\\mathrm{d} t}$?\n\nIf $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as **differentiating under the integral sign**.\n\nThe integral represents the total area under the curve $y = f(x,t).$ Changing the parameter $t$ alters the height of the curve at each $x,$ but the interval $[a,b]$ remains fixed.\nThus, the total change in area is obtained by summing (integrating) how each point changes.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} e^{-tx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-tx}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}({e}^{-tx})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-x{e}^{-tx}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods. For the purposes of integration, we treat $t$ as a constant." 13194139534774 17592186045417 false] [17592186230184 1064 "Consider the integral\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the integration variable and $t$ is a parameter. How do we compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} I}{\\mathrm{d} t}$?\n\nIf $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as **differentiating under the integral sign**.\n\nThe integral represents the total area under the curve $y = f(x,t).$ Changing the parameter $t$ alters the height of the curve at each $x,$ but the interval $[a,b]$ remains fixed.\nThus, the total change in area is obtained by summing (integrating) how each point changes.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} e^{-tx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-tx}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}({e}^{-tx})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-x{e}^{-tx}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods. For the purposes of integration, we treat $t$ as a constant." 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186230220 1064 "Now, given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle I(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nlet's calculate $I'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ by *differentiating under the integral sign*.\n\nRecall that if $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(3x-t^2)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-4t(3x-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{'}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-4t(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4t{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0-2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{'}(t) & =-4t(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 false] [17592186230220 1064 "Now, given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle I(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nlet's calculate $I'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ by *differentiating under the integral sign*.\n\nRecall that if $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(3x-t^2)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-4t(3x-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-4t(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4t{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0-2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-4t(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186311549 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 false] [17592186311549 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435663 1080 "40+4t" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435663 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435663 1083 #uuid "fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435664 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{2t}+e^{4t}" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435664 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435664 1083 #uuid "fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435665 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435663 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435665 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435665 1077 "field-1" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435665 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435663 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435665 1079 #uuid "3a8abd96-4c9a-53da-b4f4-8bc3009cd780" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435664 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435666 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435666 1077 "field-1" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435666 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435664 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435666 1079 #uuid "9450d3d5-ae63-5af3-bb4d-54253eb9dcc6" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435666 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435667 1069 "q-352881" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435667 1071 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435667 1072 #uuid "a0a5fd86-1340-5688-ba59-8ec4e8026e82" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435667 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}] & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{4t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}).\n$$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435665 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435668 1069 "q-334938" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435668 1071 "Given that $J(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${J}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${J}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435668 1072 #uuid "c52ae607-3210-5388-a941-db6a09d117b2" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true] [17592186435668 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}] & =2(5x+t)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(5x+t) \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}5x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{3}+tx{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =20+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2(20+2t) \\\\  & =4(t+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534774 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e" 17592186435663 "answer-fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea" 17592186435664 "answer-field-3a8abd96-4c9a-53da-b4f4-8bc3009cd780" 17592186435665 "answer-field-9450d3d5-ae63-5af3-bb4d-54253eb9dcc6" 17592186435666 "question-a0a5fd86-1340-5688-ba59-8ec4e8026e82" 17592186435667 "question-c52ae607-3210-5388-a941-db6a09d117b2" 17592186435668}}
